# Line endings
# ============
# This repo is edited from a Windows editor (writes CRLF) and from tooling that
# writes LF. With no rules, a third of the tree showed as modified with nothing
# actually changed — 214 files at one point, every one of them a carriage return
# and not a line of content. That noise masked whether a real change was sitting
# in the tree, which is the failure mode worth preventing.
#
# `text=auto` makes git normalize to LF on the way into the repo, so the
# difference stops reaching `git status` at all, whatever an editor writes
# locally. `eol=lf` checks files out as LF to match the two-thirds of the tree
# that already was, and the JS/Electron toolchain's defaults.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files must keep CRLF or cmd.exe can mis-parse them
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Explicit binaries — never normalize, never diff as text
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.pdf   binary
*.xlsm  binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.xls   binary
*.docx  binary
*.pptx  binary
*.dat   binary
*.zip   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.node  binary
